or herself it's a  little different in different stories  but we know what's supposed to come next  so when Jesus says this time not yet  it's actually not yours and it's not  fair for me to heal you it totally  disrupts everything that we were  expecting I mean don't you feel cheated  where is the healing Jesus this is not  how the story is supposed to go this  isn't the gospel and the good news that  we know well it turns out that the good  news that the time is now and God is  near is only good news for Galilee not  for tyre not for women from Sarah  phoenicia I imagine for Mark's original  audience this kind of cut two ways for  all of the Gentile converts who were  suddenly making claims to this new and  bold covenant it took them down a notch  right it's a reminder that you know  actually this covenant doesn't belong to  you you weren't even a part of it it  kind of disrupts I guess any claims to  self righteousness or exclusion and then  for the Jews the members of that early  church it affirms their primary claim to  God's promised Kingdom and it reassures  their place as the children of God but I  also imagine that if any of those Jewish  listeners were feeling particularly smug  and self-righteous themselves after  hearing it they would be surprised to  find out the  that's not the end of the story mark it  turns out is setting them up as well so  if you keep reading a little ways we  find that we get tripped up on this  initial statement so that we start to  pay attention to where we're standing  and where we're stepping because what  comes next is even more radical even  more shocking than what came before this  woman's reaction her response to that  riddle is not to bite or to snarl or to  just go away too bad no healing for you  instead she in her position bowed down  at Jesus's feet takes on that metaphor  and expands it even wider to say even  the dogs eat the crumbs from beneath the  table this anonymous woman has humbled  herself on behalf of her b