nk it reminded me of  back in chapter 4 when Jesus says who is  my mother who are my brothers the people  who do the will of God that is my family  and here sitting before him reflecting  back to him the image of God is this  woman that he then claims as his own I  think the insult itself as shocking as  it is is just there to get us to the  next part and I don't want to dismiss it  you know scholars have spent many many  years trying to explain it and defend it  and soften it because the words out of  Jesus  mouths are so harsh the truth is I don't  know if jesus said this particular thing  to this particular woman in this  particular way but what I do know is  that it's a word of truth to that early  church which was suffering a lot of  division at the time and it's also a  word of truth for all of us today  because it disrupts any exclusive claim  that we would make bike on God it blurs  all of the lines of who's inside and  who's outside and who has access and who  doesn't it's a call for all of us to  humility it's a reminder that God's  grace doesn't belong to one group or the  other that God's promises and God's  healing are neither something that is  owed to us nor something that we own for  ourselves it's purely gift it reminds us  that we are all of us guests within  God's grace but it's also a reminder  that we ourselves are empowered to claim  our place within that covenant because  we have been invited and that we do that  by living into the kind of life that  Jesus lived by showing within our own  lives the way that Jesus made himself  last made himself servant to others  accepted the ways that he was not  accepted in the world we know that  that's the way the world works but we're  invited to be a different kind of  community and in her witness I find  great hope so friends the time indeed is  now and God is near let's get started  amen
 

